solution_md:
No need for trig.

<details><summary>Alternate Mass Point Solution</summary>[asy]
pathpen=black;
pair D=(0,0),C=(9,0),B=(-5,0),A=(0,12),F=foot(C,A,B),H=IP(C--F,A--D);
D(MP("A",A,N)--MP("B",B,W)--MP("C",C,E)--cycle);
D(C--MP("F",F,W));
D(A--MP("D",D,S));
D(rightanglemark(A,D,C,20));
D(rightanglemark(C,F,A,20));
D(MP("H",H,NE),black);
label(" $5$ ",(B+D)/2,S);
label(" $9$ ",(C+D)/2,S);
label(" $15$ ",(A+C)/2,E);
label(" $\frac{70}{13}$ ",(B+F)/2,W);
label(" $\frac{99}{13}$ ",(A+F)/2,W);[/asy]

Let  $\triangle ABC$  be the triangle.  $F$  is the foot of the altitude from  $C$  to  $AB$ . We draw  $AD$  and  $CF$ . It can easily be shown that  $AD=12$  since  $\triangle ABC$  is a  $13-14-15$  triangle. Notice that  $\triangle ADB\sim\triangle CFB$  by AA similarity. So \[\frac{5}{BF}=\frac{13}{14}\implies 13BF=70\implies BF=\frac{70}{13}\implies AF=\frac{99}{13}.\] Assign a mass of  $5$  to  $C$  and a mass of  $9$  to  $B$ . Then the mass at  $A$  is  $\tfrac{70}{11}$  and the mass at  $D$  is  $14$ . Thus, \[\frac{HD}{AH}=\frac{m(A)}{m(D)}=\frac{70/11}{14}=\boxed{\frac{5}{11}.}\]</details>

