Since  $\overline{MD}$  and  $\overline{PC}$  are parallel,  $\triangle BMD \sim \triangle BPC$ . Then,  $\frac{BM}{MP} = \frac{BD}{DC}$ . Let the area of  $\triangle BPD$  be  $BM \cdot x$ . By same height different base, the  $[CDP] = MP \cdot x$ . So,  $[BPC] = [BPD] + [CDP] = BM \cdot x + MP \cdot x = x(BM + MP) = BP \cdot x$ . Using same height different base on  $\triangle ABC$  and  $\triangle BPC$ , we get  $[ABC] = \frac{AB}{BP} \cdot [BPC] = \frac{AB}{BP} \cdot BP \cdot x = AB \cdot x$ . 

So,   $$ \frac{[BPD]}{[ABC]} = \frac{BM \cdot x}{AB \cdot x} = \frac{BM}{AB} = \frac{BM}{2BM} = \boxed{\frac{1}{2}} $$

